# The infinity tour: a figure-8 -- two circles joined at a point on
# their circumferences, driven as arcs, the second curving the other
# way so the robot returns to the crossing point each lap.
#
# WHY ARCS. Each segment is ONE move carrying a body distance AND a
# rotation, which the motion engine shapes end to end. Chopping the
# circles into straight chords would put an accel ramp and an end taper
# inside every chord, and the robot would stutter through a polygon
# instead of driving a curve.
#
#   TWIST vx=<v> omega=<w> angle=<a>   is an arc of radius r = v / w
#
# SIZING (2026-09-01). r = 250 mm.
#
# THE LONG AXIS IS PERPENDICULAR TO THE START HEADING -- this is the
# easy thing to get backwards and the reason the previous size did not
# fit. Starting at the crossing point facing +x and curving CCW puts
# lobe A's centre at (0, +r), so lobe A occupies y = 0..+2r; lobe B
# then occupies y = 0..-2r. The figure spans **2r in x and 4r in y**:
# 500 x 1000 mm here. Against the working budget of +-550 x +-350 mm
# (tours/FIELD.md) it fits only with its long axis along the field's
# LONG axis, so **stage the robot facing along the SHORT (N-S) axis**,
# at the field centre.
#
# The previous r = 300 was sized as though the figure were 4r x 2r the
# other way round and against the full field rather than the usable
# part; it spanned the entire 1200 mm usable width with zero margin.
#
#   v = omega * r, with omega = 57.3 deg/s = 1.0 rad/s
#   -> v = 1.0 * 250 = 250 mm/s.
#
# SEGMENT SIZE IS NOT FREE. moveX() splits any move with a nonzero
# distance and |rotation| >= 50 deg (kTurnFirstAngleRad) into a pivot
# THEN a straight -- so 90 deg arcs come out as a SQUARE, which is
# exactly what the first run of this tour drew. Each arc stays at
# 45 deg: 8 arcs per circle, still a handful of shaped moves rather
# than dozens of chords.

# PIVOT TUNING (MEASURED gopiv 2026-09-01, interleaved A/B so battery
# drift hits both arms equally). Pivot cruise 188 mm/s (omega 180 deg/s)
# beats 300 mm/s (286 deg/s) decisively: excess +1.34 deg sd 0.35 versus
# +6.88 deg sd 1.03, n=8 each. pivot_overrun was then swept AT 188 mm/s
# and 0.68 -- the value the closure sweep found at 300 mm/s -- leaves
# +1.44 deg on the table; 2.0 is the zero crossing (1.8 -> +0.29,
# 2.2 -> -0.25). pivot_overrun is speed-dependent: retune it whenever
# the pivot cruise changes.

SET twist_hold_gain 8
SET speed_floor 512
SET yaw_taper 800
SET pivot_overrun 2.0

# --- lobe A: one full circle, CCW (omega positive) -------------------
REPEAT 8

MARK lobeA
TWIST vx=250 omega=57.3 angle=45 timeout=6

END

# --- lobe B: one full circle, CW (omega negative) --------------------
REPEAT 8

MARK lobeB
TWIST vx=250 omega=-57.3 angle=-45 timeout=6

END

STOP dwell=0.5
